                <opener><seg rendition="#center">[Inclosure 1 in No.
                    847.—Translation.]</seg></opener>
                <head>FORMOSA.</head>
                <p rendition="#center"><hi rendition="#italic">Placard by “All the scholars and
                        people of Formosa.”</hi></p>
                <p rendition="#center">[From the Japan Mail.]</p>
                <p>The following is the public expression of our opinion:</p>
                <p>It appears to us that the whole of Formosa has been included in the map of China
                    for more than two hundred years; and that the several emperors of our country in
                    their successive reigns have been moderate in inflicting punishment and sparing
                    of taxation, so that the four classes of people have enjoyed happiness and have
                    grown and prospered. As to the wild aborigines behind the hills, with their
                    dwellings perched on high or burrowed in the earth, they are far from having
                    human feelings. Yet in the years which have gone by, the savage aborigines have
                    turned into tame aborigines, and the tame ones have turned into citizens; so
                    that the orderly citizens of to-day are all the wild aborigines of former
                    times.</p>
                <p rendition="#center">* * * * * * *</p>
